---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<PageLayout title="Shape morphing" slug="shape-morphing">
  <div class="monologue">
    A torus and a dodecahedron have nothing in common, except that both can say how far away they are. Blend those answers and one shape flows into the other.
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The three demos below take shapes from the geom project and morph between them, lit with the environment maps from <a href={`${base}/environment-maps`}>Environment maps</a>. Up to five shapes can be chosen; each holds for a moment, then morphs into the next, and the cycle loops. The first demo builds every shape from particles that travel between surfaces, and the second lines them up and moves them through the frame, one stopping at the centre at a time. The third ray-marches the blended shape directly.
    </p>

    <div class="subhead">Twenty-three shapes, one interface</div>
    <p>
      The shapes come from three families in geom. <strong>Signed distance functions</strong> — sphere, rounded box, box frame, torus, capped torus, link, hexagonal prism, capped cone, death star, cut hollow sphere, pyramid and rhombus — are Inigo Quilez's primitives, with proportions close to geom's. <strong>Platonic solids</strong> — tetrahedron, octahedron, dodecahedron and icosahedron — come from geom's Platonic duals demo. <strong>Implicit surfaces</strong> — gyroid, Schwarz P, tanglecube, Chmutov {"\\( T_4 \\)"}, harmonic sphere, pulsing torus and oscillating spheroid — are geom's scalar fields, several of them animated as in its moving-field demo.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both demos need one thing from a shape: the signed distance {"\\( d(p) \\)"} from a point to its surface, negative inside. The first two families provide it directly. An implicit surface only provides a field {"\\( f \\)"} whose zero set is the surface, and the size of {"\\( f \\)"} has no fixed meaning. Dividing by the gradient turns it into a distance estimate, exact near the surface where {"\\( f \\)"} is close to linear:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">{"\\[ d(p) \\approx \\frac{f(p)}{|\\nabla f(p)|} \\]"}</div>
    <div class="page-note">
      <strong>Triply periodic surfaces.</strong> The gyroid and Schwarz P fill all of space, so both are intersected with a ball: {"\\( \\max(d, |p| - 0.9) \\)"}. The gyroid is used as a thin sheet, {"\\( |d| - 0.03 \\)"}, and Schwarz P as a solid.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Gradients.</strong> Every gradient here is a tetrahedral difference: four samples at {"\\( p + \\varepsilon k_i \\)"} with {"\\( k_i \\)"} the corners of a tetrahedron, one fewer than central differences need. An implicit shape's distance therefore costs five evaluations of {"\\( f \\)"}.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Placement.</strong> All shapes live in a shape space where they fit a ball of radius about 0.9. <em>Spin</em> turns that space about its own vertical axis, <em>Tilt</em> leans it toward the camera so rings and links don't sit edge-on, and <em>Shape size</em> scales it into the world.
    </div>

    <div class="subhead">The timeline</div>
    <p>
      With shapes {"\\( S_1 \\ldots S_n \\)"} chosen, each cycle lasts <em>Hold</em> plus <em>Morph</em> seconds. During the hold the scene is the current shape; during the morph it blends from {"\\( S_k \\)"} toward {"\\( S_{k+1} \\)"}, and the last shape morphs back into the first. The blend weight follows smootherstep, so the motion starts and ends at rest:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">{"\\[ s = 6x^5 - 15x^4 + 10x^3, \\qquad x = \\frac{\\text{time into the morph}}{\\text{Morph}} \\]"}</div>
    <p>
      Like everything on this site, the timeline is a pure function of time, so recordings reproduce it frame for frame.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="demo-section" id="particle-morph">
    <div class="demo-header">
      <h2>Particle morph</h2>
      <a href={`${base}/demos/shape-particles/index.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open full size ↗</a>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-embed">
      <iframe src={`${base}/demos/shape-particles/index.html?embedded`} title="Particle morph" scrolling="no"></iframe>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        To cover a surface with particles, each particle starts from a fixed random seed point in a ball around the shape and walks to the nearest point of the surface. One step moves the point against the distance gradient by the distance itself; for an exact distance function a single step lands on the surface, and a few more fix up estimates and corners:
      </p>
      <div class="math-block">{"\\[ q \\leftarrow q - d(q)\\,\\frac{\\nabla d(q)}{|\\nabla d(q)|} \\]"}</div>
      <p>
        Every particle does this twice, once for each shape in the current morph, from the same seed. Its position is the blend of the two projections. Because both start from the same seed, a particle on the left of the torus ends up on the left of the dodecahedron: the morph moves particles over short distances instead of shuffling them across the scene. While the blend runs, curl noise from the <a href={`${base}/noise-flow#plume`}>plume</a> lifts the particles off the surface in proportion to {"\\( \\sin(\\pi s) \\)"}, so the cloud loosens between shapes and settles onto the next one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest is the environment-lit pipeline from the previous page: a position pass writes every particle into a float texture, a shadow map from the environment's sun, and spheres shaded from the map.
      </p>
      <div class="page-note">
        <strong>Density.</strong> Seeds are uniform in a ball, but projection is not area-preserving. Every seed in the region nearest a convex corner or edge lands on that corner or edge, so the edges of a box or a dodecahedron collect more particles than its faces. Inside a shape, seeds project onto the nearest part of the surface, so thin parts (a link, a box frame) are covered evenly while bulky shapes are denser on their outer surface.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Cost.</strong> With <em>Projection steps</em> {"\\( k \\)"}, a particle evaluates {"\\( 5k \\)"} distances per shape (one value and four gradient samples per step), or {"\\( 25k \\)"} field samples for an implicit surface. During a hold only one shape is projected.
      </div>
      <details>
        <summary>Controls</summary>
        <div class="shape-desc">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Background</strong> (top of the panel) — draws the backdrop. Off leaves every pixel behind the subject transparent, and the floor fades out to transparent rather than into the map; frame-accurate PNG recordings keep that alpha channel for compositing, while MP4 shows it as black.</li>
            <li><strong>Environment, Shading, Lookup, Blur, Exposure, Sun strength, Occlusion, Rotation, Camera orbit, Backdrop</strong> — the environment lighting, as on <a href={`${base}/environment-maps#geom`}>Environment maps</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Method, Count, Radius</strong> — sphere drawing method, particle count (4K to 2M) and largest sphere radius.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed</strong> — playback rate of the timeline, spin and animated shapes.</li>
            <li><strong>Shape 1–5</strong> — the shapes to cycle through, in order. Slots 2–5 can be None; with a single shape there is no morph.</li>
            <li><strong>Hold (s)</strong> — how long each shape stays still before morphing.</li>
            <li><strong>Morph (s)</strong> — how long each morph takes.</li>
            <li><strong>Shape size</strong> — world size of the shapes.</li>
            <li><strong>Spin (°/s)</strong> — rotation of the shapes about their own vertical axis.</li>
            <li><strong>Tilt (°)</strong> — lean of the shapes toward the camera.</li>
            <li><strong>Swirl</strong> — how far curl noise lifts the particles mid-morph. 0 makes the particles slide straight from one surface to the other.</li>
            <li><strong>Projection steps</strong> — gradient steps toward each surface. One is enough for exact distance functions; implicit surfaces and sharp edges need more.</li>
            <li><strong>Map size, Filter, Ground, Show env map</strong> — shadow map resolution, PCF kernel, the floor, and the map inset.</li>
            <li><strong>Find max, Record, UI, Canvas size</strong> — as on <a href={`${base}/particle-rendering#impostors`}>Particle rendering</a>; drag orbits the camera and scroll zooms.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="demo-section" id="conveyor">
    <div class="demo-header">
      <h2>Morph conveyor</h2>
      <a href={`${base}/demos/shape-conveyor/index.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open full size ↗</a>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-embed">
      <iframe src={`${base}/demos/shape-conveyor/index.html?embedded`} title="Morph conveyor" scrolling="no"></iframe>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The same particles, laid out as a procession. One shape stands at the centre of the frame, with its neighbours at the sides — above and below on a portrait canvas. At every morph the whole row moves one place along: a new shape slides in from the left, each shape moves one place to the right while it turns into the next shape in the list, and the rightmost one slides out. With <em>Swirl</em> gone, every particle moves in a straight line, from its point on one shape at one place to its point on the next shape at the next place.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four groups of particles are enough to keep the row going. Every fourth particle belongs to the same group, and the groups take turns at five places: off-screen before the row, the left side, the centre, the right side, and off-screen after it. The off-screen places lie far enough out that a shape there is wholly outside the view, so a shape stays whole until it has left the frame, and the group that left the far end jumps back to the start unseen, ready to come in again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Neighbouring places show different shapes from the list, a fixed number of steps apart. If the centre shows shape {"\\( i \\)"}, the right side shows {"\\( i - k \\)"} and the left side {"\\( i + k \\)"}, where {"\\( k \\)"} is the <em>Phase offset</em>. For each moving shape to arrive one step further on, the centre has to advance {"\\( k + 1 \\)"} shapes per move:
      </p>
      <div class="math-block">{"\\[ \\text{place } m = S_{(k+1)c - km}, \\qquad m = -1, 0, 1 \\]"}</div>
      <p>
        Here {"\\( c \\)"} counts the moves so far. With the default {"\\( k = 1 \\)"} and five shapes, the three visible shapes are always different, and the centre visits every shape within five moves.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two effects sit on top. The frame is drawn into an offscreen texture and blurred by an amount that grows with the distance from the centre, so the side shapes go soft while the centre stays sharp. And each sphere's environment lookup is turned by the shape's surface normal at that particle. The spheres keep their own shading, but which part of the environment they show follows the form of the shape, so faces that point different ways pick up different hues.
      </p>
      <div class="page-note">
        <strong>Layout.</strong> The row runs along the camera's right vector when the canvas is landscape or square and along its down vector when it is portrait, so it stays aligned with the screen while the camera orbits. Neighbours are <em>Shape spacing</em> times the centre-to-edge distance apart, independent of <em>Shape scale</em>, and the shapes shrink with the view's long side so the side shapes stay partly in frame on squarer canvases. The off-screen places are one more spacing out, and a shape there must clear the side of the view frustum: with {"\\( a \\)"} the half-angle of the view along the row and {"\\( X \\)"} the distance at which the frustum's side crosses the shapes' plane, the centre of the shape's bounding ball has to be more than {"\\( R / \\cos a \\)"} past {"\\( X \\)"}. When a large shape needs more room, only the off-screen places move further out, so the shapes entering and leaving travel a little faster than the others. A vertical row would pass through the floor, so the floor is hidden on portrait canvases, and the shadow frustum is fitted to the whole row in every direction it can run.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Groups.</strong> At the hold of move {"\\( c \\)"}, group {"\\( g \\)"} stands at place {"\\( ((g + c) \\bmod 4) - 2 \\)"}, where −2 is off-screen before, −1, 0 and 1 are the visible places, and 2 is off-screen after. A group parked off-screen during the hold skips the projection entirely.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Blur.</strong> Distance from the centre is measured with the long edge at 1. Each pixel gathers 48 taps on a golden-angle spiral over a disc whose radius rises from zero at <em>Sharp radius</em> to <em>Edge blur</em> at the long edge, with a smoothstep in between. The offscreen texture is mipmapped, and the taps read the mip level whose texels match the spacing between taps, so wide discs stay smooth. The radius is set in pixels at 1080 lines and scales with the canvas, so 2× recordings blur the same.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Surface normal.</strong> The position pass writes each particle's shape normal — the normalised SDF gradient at its projected point, blended between the two shapes during a morph — to a second render target. The sphere shader takes the direction toward the camera, {"\\( a \\)"}, and the surface normal {"\\( N \\)"}, and rotates the sphere's lookup normal about {"\\( a \\times N \\)"} by the angle between them times <em>Turn toward surface normal</em>. At ×1 the sphere's camera-facing point samples along {"\\( N \\)"}; at ×2 the visible half of the shape's normals covers the whole environment, which spreads the hues around the full wheel. Sun, specular and shadow still use the sphere's own normal.
      </div>
      <details>
        <summary>Controls</summary>
        <div class="shape-desc">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Background, Lighting, Particles, Shadows</strong> — as in the first demo. Here the background and the ground start off, and the environment turns at 10°/s.</li>
            <li><strong>Shape scale</strong> — size of the shapes as a multiple of the size that fits the frame.</li>
            <li><strong>Shape 1–5, Hold, Morph, Spin, Tilt, Projection steps</strong> — as in the first demo. <em>Hold</em> is how long the row stands still between moves; <em>Morph</em> is how long a move takes.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase offset</strong> — how many shapes apart in the list neighbouring places are, from 1 to the number of shapes minus one.</li>
            <li><strong>Shape spacing</strong> — distance between neighbouring shapes, as a fraction of the distance from the centre to the edge of the frame. <em>Shape scale</em> does not change it.</li>
            <li><strong>Turn toward surface normal</strong> — how far each sphere's environment lookup turns toward the shape's surface normal: 0 for plain spheres, ×1 to look along the normal, more to spread the colours further.</li>
            <li><strong>Edge blur</strong> — blur radius at the long edge of the frame, in pixels at 1080 lines; Off renders without the blur pass.</li>
            <li><strong>Sharp radius</strong> — how far from the centre the frame stays sharp, with the long edge at 1.</li>
            <li><strong>Find max, Record, UI, Canvas size</strong> — as in the first demo. The canvas size picks the layout: a horizontal row for landscape and square, a vertical one for portrait.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="demo-section" id="sdf-morph">
    <div class="demo-header">
      <h2>SDF morph</h2>
      <a href={`${base}/demos/shape-sdf/index.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open full size ↗</a>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-embed">
      <iframe src={`${base}/demos/shape-sdf/index.html?embedded`} title="SDF morph" scrolling="no"></iframe>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        With distance functions, the morph itself is one line: blend the two distances.
      </p>
      <div class="math-block">{"\\[ d_s(p) = (1 - s)\\,d_A(p) + s\\,d_B(p) \\]"}</div>
      <p>
        The surface of the blend is where the weighted distances cancel. Away from both shapes it bulges where either one does; a hole that only one shape has shrinks and closes as {"\\( s \\)"} grows. No correspondence between the shapes is needed, and the topology changes freely: the torus's hole fills in, the gyroid's tunnels open out of a solid.
      </p>
      <p>
        A fragment shader draws it by sphere tracing. Each pixel's ray starts where it enters a bounding sphere and steps forward by the distance the field reports, which is always safe for an exact distance. A blend of distances, and an implicit surface's estimate, can overstate the true distance, so every step is shortened to 0.8 of the reported value. The normal is the field's gradient at the hit point, and shading is the same <code>envShade()</code> the particles use.
      </p>
      <div class="subhead">Rendering methods from geom</div>
      <p>
        The hit point can be shaded four ways, each carried over from geom's lighting experiments. <strong>Environment map</strong> is the lighting from the previous page. <strong>Studio</strong> is geom's standard look, the white material used across this site: a key light, a fill light, a Blinn-Phong highlight and a cool Fresnel rim, with the soft shadow taken from the key. <strong>Rim light + SSS</strong> lights only the silhouette, through {"\\( (1 - |n \\cdot v|)^p \\)"} for exponent {"\\( p \\)"}, and leaves faces facing the camera dark. <strong>Flash light</strong> is two lights orbiting the shape at different speeds. In geom, the left and right audio channels set their brightness; here each one flashes on a clock and decays, and the first never goes fully dark.
      </p>
      <p>
        Subsurface scattering in geom is a thickness measurement. From the hit point, a second ray keeps marching through the inside of the shape until the field turns positive again at the back face. Light passes through thin material more easily, so the glow is {"\\( e^{-\\text{thickness} \\times \\text{density}} \\)"}, scaled by <em>SSS strength</em>. Rim light uses it as its only fill; Studio and Flash add it in white, and Environment map in the map's colour along the normal, as geom's environment demo did. Thin sheets such as the gyroid, the box frame and the edges of a morph glow; a sphere's thick middle stays dark.
      </p>
      <div class="page-note">
        <strong>Soft shadows.</strong> A shadow ray marches toward the light through the same field: the environment's sun, or the key light in Studio. Rim and flash lighting cast none. Instead of a yes/no answer it keeps the smallest ratio {"\\( k\\,d/t \\)"} of distance to travelled length — how narrowly the ray misses the shape — which approximates a penumbra (Inigo Quilez's soft-shadow method). <em>Shadow sharpness</em> is {"\\( k \\)"}. The ground marches a shadow ray too, but only when it passes through the bounding sphere.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Ambient occlusion.</strong> Five samples step out along the normal; where the field reports less distance than the step, nearby surface is crowding the point. The result darkens creases and the inside of tunnels.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Cost.</strong> Every pixel inside the bounding sphere may run up to <em>March steps</em> evaluations of two shapes, plus four for the normal, up to 64 for the shadow and five for occlusion. Implicit shapes cost five field samples per evaluation, so morphs involving them are the slowest. The demo renders at the full canvas resolution chosen in the top bar.
      </div>
      <details>
        <summary>Controls</summary>
        <div class="shape-desc">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Environment, Shading, Lookup, Blur, Exposure, Sun strength, Occlusion, Rotation, Camera orbit, Backdrop</strong> — the environment lighting, as in the first demo. Occlusion applies to the soft shadow.</li>
            <li><strong>Background</strong> — as in the first demo; transparent pixels come out of the ray march directly.</li>
            <li><strong>Rendering</strong> (top of the panel) — Environment map, Studio, Rim light + SSS, or Flash light.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed, Shape 1–5, Hold, Morph, Shape size, Spin, Tilt</strong> — as in the first demo.</li>
            <li><strong>Rim exponent</strong> — Rim light only: {"\\( p \\)"}; higher values pull the glow closer to the silhouette.</li>
            <li><strong>Face-on fill</strong> — Rim light only: brightness of surfaces facing the camera, {"\\( |n \\cdot v|^2 \\)"} times this value.</li>
            <li><strong>SSS strength, SSS density</strong> — brightness of the thickness glow, and how fast it fades as the material gets thicker. Strength 0 skips the interior march.</li>
            <li><strong>Flash rate (/s)</strong> — Flash light only: flashes per second of the first light; the second flashes 0.618 times as often.</li>
            <li><strong>March steps</strong> — the most steps a ray may take. Too few leave holes where rays graze a surface.</li>
            <li><strong>Shadow sharpness</strong> — {"\\( k \\)"} in the soft-shadow estimate; higher values give harder shadow edges.</li>
            <li><strong>Ground, Show env map</strong> — the floor and the map inset.</li>
            <li><strong>Record, UI, Canvas size</strong> — as in the first demo.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
